    "answer": {
      "short": "One possible set has marks $2,7,12,15,20$ with frequencies $2,8,12,6,2$, respectively. Add 5 marks to six students on 12 and to all six students on 15. The mean increases by 2 marks and the median stays at 12 marks.",
      "worked": "Use marks $2,7,12,15,20$ with frequencies $2,8,12,6,2$, respectively. The first two classes contain $10$ marks altogether, so the 15th and 16th marks are both $12$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}n &= 2+8+12+6+2\\\\n &= 30.\\end{align*}$$\nGive $5$ bonus marks each to six students who scored $12$ and all six who scored $15$. Their new marks are $17$ and $20$, respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Increase in total} &= 12\\times5\\\\\\text{Increase in total} &= 60\\text{ marks}.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Increase in mean} &= \\frac{60}{30}\\\\\\text{Increase in mean} &= 2\\text{ marks}.\\end{align*}$$\nTen marks are still below $12$ and six marks are still equal to $12$. The 15th and 16th marks therefore remain $12$, so the median is unchanged.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
